G5207.b:
⟪υἱός⟫, -οῦ, ὁ, [in LXX very freq. and nearly always for ⁨בֵּן⁩, ⟦Genesis 4:17|Ge 4:17⟧, al.; for ⁨בַּר⁩, ⟦Daniel 7:13|Da LXX TH 7:13⟧, al.; etc. ;], ⟪a son⟫;
⟪1.⟫ in the ordinary sense: ⟦Matthew 10:37|Mt 10:37⟧, ⟦Mark 9:17|Mk 9:17⟧, ⟦Luke 1:13|Lk 1:13⟧, al. mult.; omitted with the art. of origin (WM, § 30, 3; Bl., §35, 2), τὸν τοῦ Ἰεσσαί, ⟦Acts 13:22|Ac 13:22⟧ (LXX); also c. gen. anarth. (cl.), Σώπατρος Πύρρου Βεροιαῖος, ⟦Acts 20:4|Ac 20:4⟧; c. adj., προτότοκος, ⟦Luke 2:7|Lk 2:7⟧; μονογένης, ⟦Luke 7:12|Lk 7:12⟧; opp. to νόθος, ⟦Hebrews 12:8|He 12:8⟧; in a wider sense, of posterity: ὁ υἱ. Δαυΐδ, of the Messiah (cf. Dalman, Words, 316 ff.; DCG, ii, 653f .), ⟦Matthew 22:42|Mt 22:42⟧, ⟦Matthew 22:45|45⟧ ⟦Mark 12:35|Mk 12:35⟧, ⟦Mark 12:37|37⟧ ⟦Luke 20:41|Lk 20:41⟧, ⟦Luke 20:44|44⟧ al.; υἱοὶ Ἰσραήλ, (cf. υἷες Ἀχαιῶν, Hom., Il., i, 162, al.), ⟦Matthew 27:9|Mt 27:9⟧, ⟦Acts 9:15|Ac 9:15⟧, al.
⟪2.⟫ Metaph.;
	⟪(a)⟫ as belonging to, being connected with or having the quality of that which follows (a usage mainly due to translation from a Semitic original; cf. Deiss., BS, 161ff.; Dalman, Words, 115 f.; DCG, ii, 652f.): τ. πονμροῦ (διαβόλου), ⟦Matthew 13:38|Mt 13:38⟧, ⟦Acts 13:10|Ac 13:10⟧; τ. νυμφῶνος (v.s. νυμφών), ⟦Matthew 9:15|Mt 9:15⟧, ⟦Mark 2:19|Mk 2:19⟧, al.; τ. φωτός (Lft., Notes, 74), ⟦Luke 16:8|Lk 16:8⟧, ⟦John 12:36|Jo 12:36⟧, ⟦1 Thessalonians 5:5|I Th 5:5⟧; τ. εἰρήνης, ⟦Luke 10:6|Lk 10:6⟧; γεέννης, ⟦Matthew 23:15|Mt 23:15⟧; τ. ἀπωλείας, ⟦John 17:12|Jo 17:12⟧, ⟦2 Thessalonians 2:3|II Th 2:3⟧; τ. αἰῶνος τούτου, ⟦Luke 16:8|Lk 16:8⟧ ⟦Luke 20:34|20:34⟧; τ. ἀπειθειάς, ⟦Ephesians 2:2|Eph 2:2⟧ ⟦Ephesians 5:6|5:6⟧; βροντῆς, ⟦Mark 3:17|Mk 3:17⟧; τ. ἀναστάσεως, ⟦Luke 20:36|Lk 20:36⟧; παρακλήσεως, ⟦Acts 4:36|Ac 4:36⟧; τ. προφητῶν κ. τ. διαθήκης, ⟦Acts 3:25|Ac 3:25⟧;
	⟪(b)⟫ υἱὸς τ. θεοῦ (cf. Dalman, Words, 268 ff.; Deiss., BS, 166 f.; DB, iv, 570 ff.; DCG, ii, 654 ff.), of men, as partakers of the Divine nature and of the life to come: ⟦Matthew 5:9|Mt 5:9⟧, ⟦Luke 20:36|Lk 20:36⟧, ⟦Romans 8:14|Ro 8:14⟧ ⟦Romans 9:26|9:26⟧, al.; υἱοὶ (κ. θυγατέρες) τ. ὑψίστου, ⟦Luke 6:35|Lk 6:35⟧, ⟦2 Corinthians 6:18|II Co 6:18⟧; in an unique sense of Jesus, ⟦Matthew 4:3|Mt 4:3⟧ ⟦Matthew 8:29|8:29⟧ ⟦Matthew 28:19|28:19⟧, ⟦Mark 3:4|Mk 3:4⟧, ⟦Luke 4:41|Lk 4:41⟧, ⟦John 9:35|Jo 9:35⟧ ⟦John 11:27|11:27⟧, al.; ὁ Χριστὸς ὁ υἱ. τ. θεοῦ ζῶντος (τ. εὐλογητοῦ), ⟦Matthew 16:16|Mt 16:16⟧, ⟦Mark 14:61|Mk 14:61⟧;
	⟪(c)⟫ (ὁ) υἱὸς τοῦ ἀνθρώπου (in LXX for Heb. ⁨בּן אדם⁩, Aram, ⁨בּר אנשׁ⁩; cf. Dalman, Words, 234 ff.; DB, iv, 579 ff.; DCG, ii, 659 ff.; Westc., St. John, i, 74 ff.; other reff. in Swete, Mk, ⟦Mark 2:10|2:10⟧), based on the Aram. of ⟦Daniel 7:13|Da 7:13⟧, where the phrase, like the corresponding Heb. (as in ⟦Psalms 8:5|Ps 8:5⟧), means ⟪a man⟫, one of the species, and indicates the human appearance of the person in question. It is used of the Messiah in Enoch, c. 46, §1-4, also in ⟦Ezra 13:3|II Es 13:3⟧, ⟦Ezra 13:12|12⟧ , al. Our Lord first makes the phrase a title, using the def. art. It seems to combine the ideas of his true humanity and representative character. Exc. in ⟦Acts 7:56|Ac 7:56⟧ and (anarth.) ⟦Revelation 1:13|Re 1:13⟧ ⟦Revelation 14:14|14:14⟧, it is used of Jesus only by himself: ⟦Matthew 8:20|Mt 8:20⟧, ⟦Mark 2:10|Mk 2:10⟧, ⟦Luke 5:24|Lk 5:24⟧, ⟦John 1:52|Jo 1:52⟧, al.

G5215.b:
⟪ὕμνος⟫, -ου, ὁ
[in LXX for ⁨תְּהִלָּה⁩, ⁨נְגִינָה⁩, ⁨שִׁיר⁩, etc. ;] ⟪a hymn⟫;
⟪(a)⟫ in cl. a festal song in praise of gods or heroes;
⟪(b)⟫ in LXX and NT a song of praise addressed to God: ⟦Ephesians 5:19|Eph 5:19⟧, ⟦Colossians 3:16|Col 3:16⟧.†
SYN.: ψαλμός, that which is sung to a musical accompaniment; ᾠδή, the generic term for song (cf. Tr., Syn., § lxxviii, Lft. on ⟦Colossians 3:16|Col 3:16⟧)

G5260.b:
** ⟪ὑπο-βάλλω⟫
[in LXX: ⟦Daniel 3:9|Da TH 3:9⟧ A, I Es 2:18 * ;] ⟪to throw⟫ or ⟪put under⟫. Metaph.,
⟪(a)⟫ ⟪to subject, submit⟫;
⟪(b)⟫ ⟪to suggest, whisper, prompt⟫;
⟪(c)⟫ ⟪to suborn⟫ (v. Field, Notes, 113), ⟪instigate⟫: c. acc pers., ⟦Acts 6:11|Ac 6:11⟧.†

G5265.b:
⟪ὑπο-δέω⟫
[in LXX: ⟦2 Chronicles 28:15|II Ch 28:15⟧, ⟦Ezekiel 16:10|Ez 16:10⟧ (⁨נָעַל⁩) * ;] ⟪to bind under⟫, esp. of foot gear. Most freq. in mid, and pass. c. acc;
⟪(a)⟫ of the foot, ὑποδησάμενοι τ. πόδας, ⟪your feet shod⟫: ⟦Ephesians 6:15|Eph 6:15⟧;
⟪(b)⟫ of that which is put on, σανδάλια: ⟦Mark 6:9|Mk 6:9⟧, ⟦Acts 12:8|Ac 12:8⟧.†
